trend yet.\n\nI'm checking the veer estimate against a rough guess, which lands around 3 to 5. The fall time of 7.15 s from 100 m is well above the 4.5 s free fall would give, so drag must be significant, and I'm thinking about how terminal velocity relates to it."}, {"before_call": 6, "text": "I'm comparing the predicted and observed fall times, and the predictions run long, so I'm weighing whether drag should come down. The westward drift also doesn't match, so I'll try a drag value of 0.025."}, {"before_call": 7, "text": "I'm narrowing in on the drag value, and the interpolated zero for the three tests lands around 0.027, so I'll compare at that point."}, {"before_call": 8, "text": "I'm working out the updraft case now: dropping the load through the smoke over the fire at different altitudes, since the updraft changes the fall time. I'm thinking about releasing it upwind so it drifts through the center, given the wind blowing toward the SSW at around 10 m/s.\n\nI'm testing the release point with a guessed updraft of zero, then I'll check it against the real value. For a low drop at 30 m with speed 45, I'm wondering about a heading of 90 and simulating where the load lands when released from (0,0)."}, {"before_call": 9, "text": "If the release point at (-46, 38) hits the center with no updraft, an updraft would lengthen the fall and push it off course. I'll go with altitude 80, heading 90, and speed 45 from that point, and keep in mind that an updraft up to 15 would change things noticeably."}, {"before_call": 10, "text": "The measured fall time came out to 6.61 seconds, longer than the 5.99 seconds predicted with no updraft. I'll compare that gap against the updraft values."}, {"before_call": 11, "text": "The updraft looks to be around 3, so I'll leave 2.5 and 3.5 for later. Next I'm turning to the wind direction and veer: the residuals in dy are mostly positive, which suggests the prediction runs too far north and the wind may be more southward. The observed directions at those altitudes are noisy, so I want to test how sensitive things are to the updraft by trying 3.5."}, {"before_call": 12, "text": "I think the residual noise of about 1.5 m is acceptable, so the error is still fine. I'm considering more test drops at different speeds and headings to check drag, along with higher altitudes, since the targets may need different altitudes and speeds and smoke columns of varying intensity. With six drops left, I'll use some to validate the extremes, like high altitude at speed 80 on heading 0, and a low drop at altitude 30 through smoke at a different location.\n\nI'm planning a high-speed, 150 m drop through the smoke, along with a 30 m smoke drop at an off-center spot. I'm weighing whether to predict with the simulator first, but I'll just run the test drops and compare the results. Drop 5 would be heading 270, speed 80, altitude 150, released at (150, 100), and I'm not worried about whether it lands in the smoke, since it's mainly for validation."}, {"before_call": 14, "text": "For the fifth drop, I want to land near the center, so I'm looking at releasing from (134, 84) heading 270 at speed 80 and altitude 150, though it will likely clip part of the smoke. The sixth drop would release from (7, -47) heading 0, at 30 m and speed 60."}, {"before_call": 17, "text": "The measured y-offset sits about 2.6 high on average, so the predictions lean too far north. A more southward wind might explain it, but the measured directions are already around 200\u2013208, so a direction change alone doesn't fit. Perhaps the wind speed is higher, adding a larger southward component. I'll try a few combinations, starting with something near 11.2 m/s at about 203\u00b0."}, {"before_call": 18, "text": "The x-offset is mostly positive now, around +1.9, so the prediction lands too far east and I need a more westward push. Turning the wind more westward is the obvious lever, though I'm not sure that's the right direction yet. I want to compare dra